UID:bee3100f3d7fc8de@role-estate-probate.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20261123T172614Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261117
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] US Department of Labor proposes new class exe
 mption for qualified professional asset managers to protect retirement pla
 ns from disruptions
DESCRIPTION:WASHINGTON – The U.S. Department of Labor’s Employee Benef
 its Security Administration today proposed a class exemption that would st
 reamline the process for qualified professional asset managers to continue
  engaging in transactions permitted under Prohibited Transaction Class Exe
 mption 84-14 – also known as the QPAM exemption – following an ineligi
 bility event. The proposed QPAM Re-eligibility Options After Disqualifica
 tion Class Exemption\, known as QROAD\, contains safeguards to protect pla
 ns and participants through clear\, well-established conditions\, includin
 g the requirement that asset managers seeking relief under the proposed ex
 emption have an independent fiduciary certify their eligibility and compli
 ance.“For years\, QPAMs seeking to rely on the beneficial QPAM class exe
 mption following an ineligibility event have had to go through an individu
 al exemption process that can be costly\, burdensome\, and time-consuming 
 for everyone involved\,” said U.S. Secretary of Labor Keith Sonderling. 
 “The QROAD class exemption addresses that problem by creating an alterna
 tive path to relief while maintaining the high standards of integrity need
 ed to protect affected plans and participants.”Since 1984\, the QPAM exe
 mption has provided broad relief for employee benefit plan and IRA transac
 tions that otherwise would be prohibited under the Employee Retirement Inc
 ome Security Act if the transactions involved a qualified professional ass
 et manager. QPAMs are expected to maintain a high standard of integrity. 
 If the QPAM or certain of its related parties engage in ineligibility even
 ts\, such as a criminal conviction or prohibited misconduct\, it cannot re
 ly on the QPAM exemption for 10 years. The department has granted several
  individual exemptions over the years that allow QPAMs to continue to rely
  on the QPAM exemption notwithstanding such disqualification. For such exe
 mptions\, QPAMs must demonstrate that their continued reliance on the QPAM
  exemption would protect and benefit plans an\nhttp://www.dol.gov/newsroom
 /releases/ebsa/ebsa20261117
URL:http://www.dol.gov/newsroom/releases/ebsa/ebsa20261117
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:fab5f5ad43db4a30@role-estate-probate.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20261123T172614Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261116
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] US Department of Labor proposes to modernize 
 permanent labor certification recruitment\, strengthening protections for 
 American workers
DESCRIPTION:WASHINGTON – The U.S. Department of Labor today issued a pro
 posed rule that would enhance American worker protections when employers
  apply for certification to hire foreign workers permanently in the Unite
 d States. The proposed rule modernizes the Permanent Labor Certification
  program by strengthening recruitment standards and recordkeeping requirem
 ents\, eliminating administrative exemptions to the statutory labor market
  test\, and improving safeguards for all American workers\, especially tho
 se affected by layoffs. This proposed update to the PERM program – the 
 first comprehensive set of regulatory reforms to the program in nearly 20 
 years – will ensure that highly qualified American workers receive fair 
 consideration and first access to job opportunities.“American Workers ar
 e the best in the world\, and they deserve the first and best job opportun
 ities. Existing regulations have instead allowed companies to sponsor fore
 ign workers without fully considering American workers first\,” said Sec
 retary of Labor Keith Sonderling. “This proposed rule change will ensure
  American workers are first in line for high-skilled\, high-paying job opp
 ortunities over foreign labor.”The proposed rule would require employers
  to level the playing field when recruiting workers by using the same chan
 nels and methods when advertising jobs. Proposed changes include a require
 ment to replace print-based job advertisements with online recruiting thro
 ugh employer websites\, online job boards\, social media\, job fairs\, and
  campus recruitment. Employers would also be required to advertise positio
 ns for at least 90 calendar days and demonstrate that they made good-faith
  efforts to recruit qualified American workers. Under the proposal\, emplo
 yers would be prohibited from tailoring job opportunities to foreign worke
 rs. The department also proposed to eliminate the administrative exemptio
 n employers used to bypass the statutory labor market test for certain occ
 upations. Under the proposal\, employers recrui\nhttp://www.dol.gov/newsro
 om/releases/eta/eta20261116
URL:http://www.dol.gov/newsroom/releases/eta/eta20261116
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:a4edf065f9b1c458@role-estate-probate.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20261123T172614Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261116
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] US Department of Labor applauds federal appea
 ls court decision on administrative exhaustion requirement under federal b
 enefits law
DESCRIPTION:WASHINGTON – The U.S. Department of Labor applauded a federa
 l appeals court decision allowing employees to bring legal claims directly
  to federal court if they reasonably suspect fiduciary violations in their
  employee benefit plans covered under the Employee Retirement Income Secur
 ity Act.Issued Oct. 13 by the en banc 11th U.S. Circuit Court of Appeals\,
  the unanimous decision in Bolton v. Inland Fresh Seafood overturns preced
 ent that required employees to exhaust all administrative remedies under t
 heir ERISA-covered employee benefit plans before they could go to federal 
 court. “This decision vindicates the department’s reinvigorated amicu
 s program and its focus on statutory text\,” said Solicitor of Labor Jon
 athan Berry. “ERISA neither explicitly nor implicitly requires hard-work
 ing Americans to go anywhere but federal court to vindicate their statutor
 y rights under it. By overruling its precedent that erroneously held other
 wise\, the 11th Circuit joins all but one of its sister circuits and reinf
 orces ERISA’s uniform statutory framework.”The court held that the exh
 austion requirement was not based on ERISA’s text\, saying it was an “
 atextual product of judicial creation based solely on policy reasons.” T
 he court’s opinion also quoted the department’s oral argument\, saying
  “plaintiffs or employers should not be able to contract away or force t
 heir employees to contract away their rights under ERISA.” “The decis
 ion by the 11th Circuit rightly rejects a judicially invented exhaustion 
 requirement that has no basis in ERISA’s text or Congressional intent\,
 ” said Assistant Secretary of Labor for the Employee Benefits Security A
 dministration Daniel Aronowitz. “By aligning with the law’s clear text
  and Congress's intent to provide participants and beneficiaries direct ac
 cess to federal court\, the court’s decision makes certain that ERISA’
 s protections are applied as written in the statute itself.”Employers an
 d workers can contact EBSA at askebsa.dol.gov or call 866-444-3272 to\nht
 tp://www.dol.gov/newsroom/releases/ebsa/ebsa20261116
URL:http://www.dol.gov/newsroom/releases/ebsa/ebsa20261116
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
